          "The district's economic profile — $86,150 median income, 20.7% college attainment, and 37.8 median age — is consistent with a working-age, homeowner-dominant, rural Wyoming constituency whose economic foundation likely depends on agriculture, energy, ranching, or natural resource industries. The relatively high median income masks potential volatility: rural Wyoming's economic dependence on commodity prices, energy sector stability, and agricultural viability means that even well-off homeowners face periodic economic stress tied to forces beyond state legislative control. The low college attainment (20.7%, below Wyoming's state average) and the ~13% AIAN gap together point to a constituency where educational opportunity, workforce development, and genuine engagement of Native American tribal communities in economic planning are understated but present governance needs. An incumbent who consolidates only the existing Republican base and neglects the material concerns of younger adults, agricultural families facing commodity price volatility, and the AIAN community will discover that unopposed 2024 results do not predict 2026 vulnerability.",
          "WY-HD-21 is currently represented by an unopposed Republican, which means the district has no recent contested election data to analyze for swing dynamics, persuasion patterns, or latent opposition strength. The next contested election — whether in 2026 if Erickson faces a challenger or in a future cycle — will be the real test of this seat's underlying stability. An incumbent strategy that assumes the 100% result is durable misreads the electoral environment; unopposed races are won by default, not by earning constituent confidence. Any challenger who can demonstrate to the district's working-age homeowners, agricultural families, or the ~13% AIAN population that the incumbent is not addressing their material concerns will find a constituency that, while currently voting Republican, has not been tested on whether that alignment reflects genuine satisfaction or inherited partisan loyalty."
