          { name: "Defaulting to Energy Sector Advocacy Without Diversification Strategy", tag: "avoid", why: "Wyoming's energy sector — coal, oil, natural gas, and wind — has historically dominated state and regional politics, and unopposed Republican seats often default to unconditional energy sector advocacy. However, the sector's long-term contraction, generational workforce transition, and the reality that many households in WY-SD-6 have no direct energy sector employment create a vulnerability: a legislator perceived as energy-industry-focused rather than broadly working-class-focused can be outflanked by a challenger who articulates economic diversification, workforce retraining, and community resilience in a post-carbon transition. The unopposed incumbent should use this term to articulate a more inclusive economic vision — one that protects current energy workers and communities while acknowledging workforce trends and building alternative economic pathways. Defaulting to energy-only advocacy invites future challengers to frame the incumbent as defending an industry, not the people actually living in WY-SD-6." }
