          { name: "Treating 76% as a Safe, Permanent Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 76% result in a district with 15.54% Hispanic/Latino population, 22.9% renters, meaningful college-educated professionals, and a 36.6-year median age is not a structural landslide — it is a performance result dependent on current incumbent strength and constituency alignment. If the incumbent becomes vulnerable on cost-of-living issues, Hispanic/Latino engagement, or specific local policy failures, a challenger who consolidates the 24% opposition and mobilizes persuadable swing voters in the Hispanic/Latino and renter communities could rapidly shift the margin. Complacency is the incumbent's greatest risk." },
          { name: "Assuming Economic Interests Are Aligned Across the $94k Income Bracket", tag: "avoid", why: "The $94,130 median household income masks real economic diversity: homeowners with assets are economically positioned differently from renters at the same income level, young professionals differ from older near-retirees, and households with dual incomes face different pressures than single-earner households. Legislating or campaigning as if all households at $94k income have the same priority set — or assuming that upper-middle-class status automatically generates Republican electoral loyalty — misses the specific cost-of-living concerns that can shift votes within this income bracket. Challenger positioning that identifies specific subgroups within this income level whose concerns have been neglected could be effective." }
