          { name: "Treating 100% Partisan Result as a Durable Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "Pearson won unopposed in 2024, producing a 100% result that obscures rather than reveals the district's actual competitive terrain. Unopposed elections typically suppress turnout and obscure genuine opposition; the absence of a challenger does not indicate the absence of constituency members who would consider alternatives if one existed. At $83,722 income with substantial property holdings, this constituency is sophisticated enough to evaluate on specific policy outcomes (tax burden, service delivery, economic opportunity) rather than tribal loyalty. A representative who interprets 100% as license to ignore constituent concerns or neglect active engagement is strategically misreading the signal that unopposed status sends." },
          { name: "Assuming College Attainment Gaps Reflect Partisan Alignment", tag: "avoid", why: "The 25.6% college-attainment rate in WY-SD-14 is lower than the Wyoming state average and notably lower than college-educated populations in comparable suburban or exurban districts. It is tempting to read lower college attainment as an indicator of strong partisan Republican alignment; this is analytically dangerous. Rural and exurban constituencies with lower college attainment include both traditionally Republican small-business owners and working-class households that may evaluate differently on healthcare access, economic mobility, and whether government is delivering for them. Assuming that lower college attainment automatically predicts strong partisan stability obscures the actual economic and policy concerns that drive voter behavior in working-class rural communities." }
